TARGET:
Acyclic-defense suspension refinement: after the grounded fixpoint, run a second deterministic pure-function pass that marks each IN node as either acyclic-defended (every defense path terminates in an unattacked IN node) or cycle-dependent (IN-ness essentially depends on a cycle). Committed roots that are cycle-dependent are flagged 'suspended (cycle-dependent)' at the commitment layer only — the base IN label is preserved, so Pass 1 determinism and the least-fixpoint characterization are untouched. This adds a conservative distinction the Dung semantics deliberately flattens, and gives the scheduler a principled reason to withhold commitment until an exogenous anchor breaks the cycle.

QUESTION: does the case establish a decisive fault in the target? verdict=fail iff it does.

THE CASE FOR FAIL:
src_001 introduces a second post-fixpoint pass that must trace 'every defense path' to classify each IN node as acyclic-defended or cycle-dependent. In an abstract argumentation framework, the set of defense paths to an IN node can be exponential in the size of the graph (consider a layered graph where an argument is defended by two arguments, each of which is defended by two arguments, etc.). Determining whether every such path terminates in an unattacked IN node requires evaluating all of them. Therefore, this proposed second pass is not polynomial cost, violating the problem's requirement that each proposal preserve polynomial cost.

THE DEFENCE:
The critic's exponential-path objection conflates explicit path enumeration with the polynomial-cost verification that the pass actually requires. Determining whether every defense path from an IN node terminates in an unattacked IN node does not require materializing all exponentially many paths: it reduces to a standard reachability question on the defense subgraph induced by IN nodes — namely, whether any IN node on a defense walk from the target reaches a cycle of IN nodes without encountering an unattacked IN root. That property is decidable in polynomial time by single-source reachability plus cycle detection (DFS or SCC decomposition) on the IN-induced subgraph. A node is acyclic-defended iff every IN-reachable defense chain bottoms out at an unattacked IN node; equivalently, no IN node reachable along the defense relation lies on a directed cycle within the IN subgraph unless an unattacked root is reachable on every such path. Formulating the condition as universal path termination is a semantic specification, not an algorithmic mandate; the existence of exponentially many paths does not entail exponential verification cost any more than 'all reachable nodes are marked' requires enumerating all walks. Therefore the critic has not established that the second pass is super-polynomial, and the polynomial-cost requirement is not shown to be violated.

